1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,860
For 30 years, um, digital transformation in government meant exactly one thing, which was taking a paper process and just putting it online.

2
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:17,330
Right. Exactly. Like the form becomes a web form, the queue becomes an online appointment, the certificate is suddenly a PDF, but underneath all of that,

3
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,820
the ministry's operating model stayed exactly the same.

4
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:21,760
Yeah, nothing really changed.

5
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:29,600
So if that work is still important, why is putting paper online no longer the most important work your ministry is being asked to do?

6
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:31,050
Because, well,

7
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:33,490
ministries delivering real results

8
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:36,120
are redesigning how citizens are served,

9
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:39,560
and that, you know, completely changes the operating model.

10
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,080
Okay, so it's a deeper shift.

11
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,970
Yeah, it's the new work, and there are three specific examples of this new work.

12
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:46,840
Number one is one farmer register.

13
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:47,480
Oh, got it.

14
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:55,620
Right. And this is shared by the Agriculture Ministry, the Cooperative Bank, the Input Subsidy Program, and, um, the Climate Resilience Program.

15
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,640
It basically means recognizing the same farmer the exact same way.

16
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,660
Wow. Across all those different departments.

17
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:05,840
Exactly. Which brings us to number two, which is one national identity.

18
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:12,600
This is about letting a citizen prove who they are at, well, any service entirely without paper.

19
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:14,860
That makes things a lot easier for everyone, I'd imagine.

20
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,520
Definitely. And then number three is one learner record.

21
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:21,640
So, you know, following the child from primary school all the way up to university.

22
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:26,760
Okay, that makes total sense. But when you redesign how your ministry works in this way,

23
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:28,040
um,

24
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:32,180
what new problem arises for the people leading the work?

25
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:33,720
Well, it creates this huge gap,

26
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,390
the business side. So your minister, your director general, your head of policy,

27
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:45,160
and the IT side, like your architects and engineers, they must now decide together with the exact same seriousness.

28
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,060
Because in the past, they didn't really have to do that.

29
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:51,980
Right. They didn't. In the old work, business just decided what, and IT delivered how.

30
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:55,760
But now, you know, they sit in the same meeting, but they completely miss each other.

31
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,600
Like they're speaking two totally different languages.

32
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:01,580
Exactly that. Your minister talks about citizen services and policy goals.

33
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:06,100
Mm-hmm. But your chief architect talks about systems, APIs, and data.

34
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:08,080
Yeah. I can see how that gets messy fast.

35
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:13,260
It does. And because of that, decisions do not get made well or, you know, at all.

36
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:13,780
Wow.

37
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:20,660
So how does a national enterprise architecture solve this specific communication failure?

38
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:29,920
A national enterprise architecture, or EA, gives both sides a shared language for the new work, and it really does this by providing three specific things.

39
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:31,040
Okay, what is the first one?

40
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:34,780
Number one is the agreed picture. It is literally something both sides can point at.

41
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:36,880
Oh, like a map they can both look at.

42
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:40,400
Yeah, exactly. And number two is plain words for basic terms.

43
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:45,380
So words like service, capability, data domain, they actually mean the same thing to both sides.

44
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,520
That clears up a lot of the confusion right there.

45
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:48,370
It really does.

46
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:53,000
And finally, number three is a regular forum, specifically the EA board.

47
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:55,060
That is where they actually sit together and decide.

48
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:56,860
Okay, so they have a place to hash it all out.

49
00:02:56,940 --> 00:03:00,600
In the era of digitizing paper, an EA was useful.

50
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:09,480
In the era of redesigning how the ministry works, an EA is necessary because without it, business and IT cannot have the conversation the redesign requires.

51
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:10,980
Sources are in the video description.
